This study evaluated a sparse low-montage resting-state EEG framework for identifying clinically interpretable biomarkers associated with remission outcomes in a home-based transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS) randomised sham-controlled trial in adults with major depressive disorder. Using 4-channel eyes-closed EEG, we conducted a controlled sweep of 150 successful runs across two model families, an advanced hybrid 1D CNN-LSTM and a linear support vector machine (SVM), varying window size (2--10 s) and per-fold feature budget (\texttt{inner-k} 1--70) with leave-one-participant-out group equalization and SMOTE during training.

The strongest configuration (hybrid model, 6 s windows, \texttt{inner-k=1}) achieved patient-level ROC-AUC 0.816, PR-AUC 0.579, balanced accuracy 0.786, accuracy 0.810, and MCC 0.571 over 21 held-out participants. The best SVM configuration (8 s, \texttt{inner-k=30}) reached ROC-AUC 0.510 and MCC 0.277. Notably, this performance was obtained with sparsity, selecting 1 feature per fold and yielding only 7 unique recurrent features overall, with a permutation-tested ROC-AUC advantage over chance ($p=0.008$). Hard-set overlap across folds was modest (mean pairwise Jaccard 0.190; Kuncheva index 0.187), while recurrent features showed perfect mean effect-direction consistency.

These findings show that clinically relevant patient-level discrimination can emerge from highly sparse representations of low-montage EEG. The strongest run concentrated its signal in a small recurrent biomarker set centered on temporal spectral entropy and frontal-temporal difference measures. The findings suggest a compact, interpretable biomarker that converges with previous power spectral density-based findings, supporting low-montage resting-state EEG as a basis for pretreatment stratification.
